1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,965
This is GlucoTwin, presented for J R Deva
Dattan of Team dev at Army Institute of

2
00:00:04,965 --> 00:00:06,007
Technology, Pune.

3
00:00:06,129 --> 00:00:10,038
GlucoTwin is a research workspace for
inspecting a two-hour glucose event

4
00:00:10,038 --> 00:00:10,520
estimate.

5
00:00:10,642 --> 00:00:12,789
Every patient you will see in the app is
fictional.

6
00:00:12,911 --> 00:00:15,123
It is not intended for clinical decisions.

7
00:00:15,245 --> 00:00:19,167
The question behind this prototype is simple:
when a glucose reading changes, can

8
00:00:19,167 --> 00:00:23,138
a reviewer see what information produced the
estimate and whether that information

9
00:00:23,138 --> 00:00:23,912
is still usable?

10
00:00:24,035 --> 00:00:28,260
The interface brings the current snapshot,
model output, and its evidence into the

11
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:29,136
same review flow.

12
00:00:29,258 --> 00:00:31,478
This is a proposed clinician-facing workflow.

13
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,783
We haven't conducted clinician interviews or a
usability study.

14
00:00:34,905 --> 00:00:38,667
I'll show the working replay first, including
a false positive and a missed

15
00:00:38,667 --> 00:00:39,068
episode.

16
00:00:39,190 --> 00:00:43,555
Then I'll separate the synthetic engineering
results from a small retrospective

17
00:00:43,555 --> 00:00:45,433
benchmark on public research data.

18
00:00:45,556 --> 00:00:49,888
The real benchmark exposes a substantial
false-alert burden and only a very small

19
00:00:49,888 --> 00:00:52,509
difference between fused and glucose-only
models.

20
00:00:52,632 --> 00:00:56,339
A useful research demonstration should make
three kinds of evidence easy to

21
00:00:56,339 --> 00:00:56,933
distinguish.

22
00:00:57,055 --> 00:01:01,309
The first is software behavior: whether the
application performs the operations

23
00:01:01,309 --> 00:01:02,548
its interface promises.

24
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:05,698
The second is statistical performance on a
held out dataset.

25
00:01:05,820 --> 00:01:09,542
The third is evidence that the workflow
actually helps clinicians or patients.

26
00:01:09,664 --> 00:01:13,537
This project supplies a working software
prototype and a limited statistical

27
00:01:13,537 --> 00:01:13,843
study.

28
00:01:13,965 --> 00:01:16,079
It does not supply the third kind of evidence.

29
00:01:16,201 --> 00:01:18,731
That distinction shapes the entire
presentation.

30
00:01:18,854 --> 00:01:22,950
We will look at both favorable and unfavorable
cases, and we will keep the false

31
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:25,562
alert burden visible alongside successful
warnings.

32
00:01:25,685 --> 00:01:27,284
The goal is inspectability.

33
00:01:27,407 --> 00:01:31,335
A reviewer should be able to determine the
forecast origin, the inputs, the event

34
00:01:31,335 --> 00:01:34,099
definition, and the limitations without
needing to guess.

35
00:01:34,221 --> 00:01:38,137
All names and glucose traces in the
interactive application are synthetic

36
00:01:38,137 --> 00:01:38,620
fixtures.

37
00:01:38,742 --> 00:01:43,109
This presentation uses a stock computer
generated narration voice and captions.

38
00:01:43,231 --> 00:01:46,275
It does not reproduce or impersonate the
participant's voice.

39
00:01:46,398 --> 00:01:51,669
Our target is two consecutive readings
strictly above 180 milligrams per deciliter

40
00:01:51,669 --> 00:01:53,212
in the next 120 minutes.

41
00:01:53,335 --> 00:01:55,277
Samples arrive every 15 minutes.

42
00:01:55,400 --> 00:01:59,513
Two readings span 15 minutes; we do not
describe that as 30 minutes above range.

43
00:01:59,635 --> 00:02:04,145
A current reading above 180 suppresses a new-
event estimate, even if it is only a

44
00:02:04,145 --> 00:02:05,258
single high reading.

45
00:02:05,381 --> 00:02:07,380
That is a conservative project choice.

46
00:02:07,503 --> 00:02:10,996
Missing future samples make the outcome
unknown, rather than negative.

47
00:02:11,118 --> 00:02:13,632
These choices are part of the benchmark
definition.

48
00:02:13,754 --> 00:02:18,078
The threshold has time-in-range literature
behind it, but this particular episode

49
00:02:18,078 --> 00:02:18,985
protocol is ours.

50
00:02:19,108 --> 00:02:22,054
It is not an emergency definition or a
treatment trigger.

51
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:24,160
Consider the origin at twelve o'clock.

52
00:02:24,282 --> 00:02:28,086
A value measured at twelve o'clock belongs to
the information available to the

53
00:02:28,086 --> 00:02:28,379
model.

54
00:02:28,501 --> 00:02:30,738
It is not one of the future outcome readings.

55
00:02:30,860 --> 00:02:34,337
The future window starts with twelve fifteen
and ends with fourteen hundred.

56
00:02:34,459 --> 00:02:38,472
Suppose the readings at thirteen fifteen and
thirteen thirty are both above one

57
00:02:38,472 --> 00:02:39,437
hundred and eighty.

58
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:41,747
They form a positive event for this origin.

59
00:02:41,869 --> 00:02:45,957
If only the thirteen fifteen reading is above
the threshold, with both neighboring

60
00:02:45,957 --> 00:02:49,296
readings below it, the same origin is negative
under this protocol.

61
00:02:49,419 --> 00:02:53,206
A reading exactly equal to one hundred and
eighty is not strictly above the

62
00:02:53,206 --> 00:02:53,711
threshold.

63
00:02:53,834 --> 00:02:57,874
These boundary conditions matter because small
changes in labeling can change

64
00:02:57,874 --> 00:02:58,975
reported performance.

65
00:02:59,098 --> 00:03:02,395
They also matter when explaining why a warning
was counted as false.

66
00:03:02,517 --> 00:03:06,531
We do not retrospectively redefine the outcome
to make a selected example look

67
00:03:06,531 --> 00:03:06,892
better.

68
00:03:07,014 --> 00:03:08,434
There are two evidence streams.

69
00:03:08,557 --> 00:03:12,294
The browser runs a model trained on a
deterministic synthetic generator.

70
00:03:12,417 --> 00:03:15,453
That generator uses static patient context by
design.

71
00:03:15,575 --> 00:03:19,569
Better fusion results on it demonstrate that
the software can combine the inputs;

72
00:03:19,569 --> 00:03:21,590
they cannot establish a clinical benefit.

73
00:03:21,712 --> 00:03:27,197
The separate ShanghaiT2DM feasibility
benchmark covers 100 adults and 109

74
00:03:27,197 --> 00:03:28,625
recording sessions.

75
00:03:28,747 --> 00:03:31,277
We retain every session for a person in one
split.

76
00:03:31,400 --> 00:03:34,215
Its static inputs are age and source-recorded
gender.

77
00:03:34,338 --> 00:03:38,453
We assume these were available at enrollment,
but their exact prospective timing

78
00:03:38,453 --> 00:03:39,945
isn't independently verified.

79
00:03:40,067 --> 00:03:43,989
The source paper allows some laboratory
measurements months before or after the

80
00:03:43,989 --> 00:03:44,833
sensor recording.

81
00:03:44,956 --> 00:03:49,361
We therefore exclude all undated labs, along
with treatment history and outcome-

82
00:03:49,361 --> 00:03:50,628
derived summary fields.

83
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:53,753
Real data stay outside the public app and
repository.

84
00:03:53,876 --> 00:03:58,498
The browser's fictional HbA1c fixture is dated
before replay and has no connection

85
00:03:58,498 --> 00:03:59,964
to a Shanghai participant.

86
00:04:00,087 --> 00:04:03,844
The distinction between the two evidence
streams also determines what may be

87
00:04:03,844 --> 00:04:05,179
inferred from a comparison.

88
00:04:05,302 --> 00:04:08,574
Synthetic data are valuable for controlled
engineering checks.

89
00:04:08,697 --> 00:04:13,194
They let us exercise reproducible histories,
known failure states, and code paths

90
00:04:13,194 --> 00:04:15,470
without exposing a real person's records.

91
00:04:15,593 --> 00:04:19,404
But a relationship written into the generator
can be learned by a model without

92
00:04:19,404 --> 00:04:22,636
proving that it exists with the same strength
in clinical practice.

93
00:04:22,758 --> 00:04:26,694
The real benchmark provides a separate
feasibility check, rather than a hidden

94
00:04:26,694 --> 00:04:28,308
replacement for the app's model.

95
00:04:28,430 --> 00:04:31,572
The two pipelines have different features and
history requirements.

96
00:04:31,695 --> 00:04:33,703
Their percentages should not be exchanged.

97
00:04:33,825 --> 00:04:37,571
Grouping all recording sessions for the same
person prevents a person's second

98
00:04:37,571 --> 00:04:41,268
session from appearing in a test split while
their first session was used for

99
00:04:41,268 --> 00:04:41,701
training.

100
00:04:41,823 --> 00:04:46,232
This removes one important leakage route,
although it cannot resolve every concern

101
00:04:46,232 --> 00:04:49,780
about retrospective data quality or population
representativeness.

102
00:04:49,903 --> 00:04:53,534
This is a fictional profile selected from
held-out synthetic examples.

103
00:04:53,657 --> 00:04:55,436
The timestamp is simulated time.

104
00:04:55,558 --> 00:04:58,611
The estimate uses the observations available
at that moment.

105
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:02,702
Future readings are present in the replay
fixture but stay hidden from inference

106
00:05:02,702 --> 00:05:04,389
and the chart until we reach them.

107
00:05:04,511 --> 00:05:08,282
The percentage on the right is the logistic
event model's synthetic estimate.

108
00:05:08,404 --> 00:05:12,409
The line extending into the future is a
separate persistence baseline: it holds

109
00:05:12,409 --> 00:05:14,133
the latest glucose value constant.

110
00:05:14,256 --> 00:05:17,438
The shaded band uses validation absolute
residuals.

111
00:05:17,561 --> 00:05:21,973
It describes uncertainty under the synthetic
benchmark, without promising coverage

112
00:05:21,973 --> 00:05:23,372
for an individual patient.

113
00:05:23,494 --> 00:05:26,555
These two outputs answer different questions
and can disagree.

114
00:05:26,677 --> 00:05:31,032
A flat persistence line can coexist with a
higher event estimate because the

115
00:05:31,032 --> 00:05:34,528
logistic model also uses recent variation and
static context.

116
00:05:34,650 --> 00:05:39,025
We make that separation explicit rather than
implying the chart is a trajectory

117
00:05:39,025 --> 00:05:40,576
generated by the classifier.

118
00:05:40,698 --> 00:05:43,334
Read this screen from left to right as a
review task.

119
00:05:43,456 --> 00:05:47,007
Start with the identity of the fictional
fixture and the replay timestamp.

120
00:05:47,129 --> 00:05:49,667
Then check the current glucose value and its
units.

121
00:05:49,790 --> 00:05:52,564
Check whether the feed status says the data
are fresh enough.

122
00:05:52,687 --> 00:05:55,364
Only after those checks should you inspect the
model output.

123
00:05:55,486 --> 00:05:59,748
The provenance panel provides another level of
inspection: which context belongs

124
00:05:59,748 --> 00:06:03,903
to this fixture, what observations were
available, and what model artifact was

125
00:06:03,903 --> 00:06:04,170
used.

126
00:06:04,292 --> 00:06:08,488
The current estimate is a model score
expressed as a percentage under a synthetic

127
00:06:08,488 --> 00:06:09,058
experiment.

128
00:06:09,181 --> 00:06:12,771
It should not be interpreted as a calibrated
personal medical probability.

129
00:06:12,894 --> 00:06:16,950
Likewise, the shaded trajectory band is a
descriptive residual based band.

130
00:06:17,072 --> 00:06:21,178
A viewer should not assume that every future
point for an individual falls inside

131
00:06:21,178 --> 00:06:22,851
it with a guaranteed probability.

132
00:06:22,973 --> 00:06:27,209
Separating labels, units, and output meanings
is part of making a numerical

133
00:06:27,209 --> 00:06:28,678
interface less misleading.

134
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:31,314
Advancing fifteen minutes adds one
observation.

135
00:06:31,436 --> 00:06:35,571
The current value and last-hour statistics
change, so the event estimate changes

136
00:06:35,571 --> 00:06:35,778
too.

137
00:06:35,900 --> 00:06:38,128
The future actual trace doesn't appear early.

138
00:06:38,251 --> 00:06:42,206
Reset and the time slider make this
demonstration repeatable, although moving

139
00:06:42,206 --> 00:06:45,596
backwards is a replay convenience rather than
a clinical workflow.

140
00:06:45,718 --> 00:06:48,371
This is the selected successful-warning
illustration.

141
00:06:48,493 --> 00:06:50,550
It shows one outcome that followed a warning.

142
00:06:50,672 --> 00:06:52,974
It does not represent the overall success
rate.

143
00:06:53,096 --> 00:06:57,248
Once the current value is already above range,
the interface treats that as an

144
00:06:57,248 --> 00:07:00,335
observation and stops presenting it as advance
prediction.

145
00:07:00,457 --> 00:07:03,273
Demo review: inspected inputs and replay
outcome.

146
00:07:03,395 --> 00:07:05,362
The replay is deliberately observable.

147
00:07:05,485 --> 00:07:09,485
At each step, the current measurement becomes
part of the past, the forecast

148
00:07:09,485 --> 00:07:12,275
origin advances, and the input summary is
recomputed.

149
00:07:12,397 --> 00:07:16,301
The presence of future readings inside the
fixture file does not make them valid

150
00:07:16,301 --> 00:07:17,131
inference inputs.

151
00:07:17,253 --> 00:07:21,269
The engineering requirement is to restrict the
calculation to the chosen origin.

152
00:07:21,391 --> 00:07:24,961
When the replay reaches the first high
measurement, a new event estimate is

153
00:07:24,961 --> 00:07:28,483
withheld because the current reading is
already above the event threshold.

154
00:07:28,605 --> 00:07:32,783
That behavior should not be confused with the
model changing its mind about the

155
00:07:32,783 --> 00:07:33,682
earlier forecast.

156
00:07:33,804 --> 00:07:37,264
The earlier forecast had its own origin and
its own future window.

157
00:07:37,387 --> 00:07:41,280
A review note is merely a local session
annotation for this demonstration.

158
00:07:41,402 --> 00:07:45,116
Marking a snapshot reviewed does not imply a
clinician approved it, that a

159
00:07:45,116 --> 00:07:48,829
treatment was recommended, or that any
notification was sent to a patient.

160
00:07:48,951 --> 00:07:52,795
The distinction between observing,
forecasting, and acting remains explicit.

161
00:07:52,918 --> 00:07:55,880
This warning is a false positive under our
exact label.

162
00:07:56,003 --> 00:07:59,907
A single high reading is visible, but the
required pair never forms in this

163
00:07:59,907 --> 00:08:00,271
window.

164
00:08:00,393 --> 00:08:04,319
Changing the event definition after seeing
this example would change the task.

165
00:08:04,441 --> 00:08:05,976
We keep the definition fixed.

166
00:08:06,098 --> 00:08:09,330
This selected case is a genuinely missed
synthetic episode.

167
00:08:09,452 --> 00:08:12,937
The eligible warning origins stay below the
threshold before onset.

168
00:08:13,060 --> 00:08:17,328
A low model estimate should never be read as a
guarantee that no event will occur.

169
00:08:17,450 --> 00:08:21,474
These examples were selected after aggregate
evaluation to show behavior.

170
00:08:21,596 --> 00:08:24,379
They were not used to train the model or
select its threshold.

171
00:08:24,502 --> 00:08:27,970
The false positive and missed event make
different limitations tangible.

172
00:08:28,093 --> 00:08:32,470
A false positive consumes attention even when
its displayed score is below fifty

173
00:08:32,470 --> 00:08:32,908
percent.

174
00:08:33,030 --> 00:08:37,482
An alerting rule can use a low threshold when
recall is prioritized, so a warning

175
00:08:37,482 --> 00:08:41,330
is not the same as a prediction that an event
is more likely than not.

176
00:08:41,452 --> 00:08:45,149
Conversely, a low score can precede a real
event under the chosen label.

177
00:08:45,272 --> 00:08:49,115
The missed example is useful because it
prevents the dashboard from presenting a

178
00:08:49,115 --> 00:08:50,797
reassuring percentage as certainty.

179
00:08:50,919 --> 00:08:55,158
These cases are illustrations selected from
held out synthetic examples after

180
00:08:55,158 --> 00:08:56,314
aggregate evaluation.

181
00:08:56,436 --> 00:08:58,689
They do not provide a new estimate of
accuracy.

182
00:08:58,811 --> 00:09:02,918
A few visually memorable examples can easily
distort intuition, which is why the

183
00:09:02,918 --> 00:09:05,895
aggregate metrics later in this walkthrough
are essential.

184
00:09:06,018 --> 00:09:10,310
The same fixed event definition must be
applied to the successful warning, false

185
00:09:10,310 --> 00:09:11,812
warning, and missed episode.

186
00:09:11,935 --> 00:09:13,918
Otherwise the comparison would be unfair.

187
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:16,864
The prototype withholds its forecast when the
feed is stale.

188
00:09:16,986 --> 00:09:19,957
Its 30-minute freshness limit is an
engineering policy.

189
00:09:20,079 --> 00:09:21,565
It isn't a clinical standard.

190
00:09:21,687 --> 00:09:25,826
Cold start, invalid units, and a disconnected
feed also produce explicit

191
00:09:25,826 --> 00:09:26,919
unavailable states.

192
00:09:27,041 --> 00:09:30,966
The screen must not turn missing information
into a reassuring low percentage.

193
00:09:31,089 --> 00:09:34,672
The interface requires nine observations
before showing its forecast.

194
00:09:34,794 --> 00:09:39,473
The synthetic training eligibility uses at
least five contiguous observations, and

195
00:09:39,473 --> 00:09:41,241
the real benchmark requires 24.

196
00:09:41,364 --> 00:09:44,890
Those are separate contracts, and the
documentation records the difference.

197
00:09:45,012 --> 00:09:49,337
We test input order, gaps, units, and export
parity in the model and browser code.

198
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:53,615
These scenario controls demonstrate selected
failure states; they are not a live

199
00:09:53,615 --> 00:09:54,601
sensor integration.

200
00:09:54,724 --> 00:09:57,286
An unavailable output is an important result.

201
00:09:57,409 --> 00:10:01,642
It tells the reviewer that the system cannot
support the requested computation

202
00:10:01,642 --> 00:10:02,999
under its input contract.

203
00:10:03,122 --> 00:10:07,438
Sensor dropout and disconnection are related
but distinct: a connection may appear

204
00:10:07,438 --> 00:10:10,385
to exist while its latest usable observation
is too old.

205
00:10:10,508 --> 00:10:14,098
A cold start means that the necessary history
has not yet accumulated.

206
00:10:14,221 --> 00:10:18,362
Invalid units mean the numerical value cannot
safely be interpreted under the

207
00:10:18,362 --> 00:10:19,330
expected contract.

208
00:10:19,452 --> 00:10:23,838
The scenario selector makes these states easy
to inspect, but it is not proof that

209
00:10:23,838 --> 00:10:27,475
the application connects to or validates a
physical glucose monitor.

210
00:10:27,597 --> 00:10:31,475
The freshness rule, history requirement, and
unit checks are engineering choices

211
00:10:31,475 --> 00:10:32,396
for this prototype.

212
00:10:32,518 --> 00:10:36,582
They need to be documented and tested, and
they would need a separate review for a

213
00:10:36,582 --> 00:10:37,970
future clinical application.

214
00:10:38,092 --> 00:10:42,379
A blank or unavailable state must remain
visibly different from a low estimated

215
00:10:42,379 --> 00:10:43,030
event score.

216
00:10:43,152 --> 00:10:46,564
The running app is a React and TypeScript
application built with Vite.

217
00:10:46,686 --> 00:10:50,904
Browser inference uses exported synthetic
logistic coefficients and scaling

218
00:10:50,904 --> 00:10:51,297
values.

219
00:10:51,420 --> 00:10:53,958
A Python service is not required at runtime.

220
00:10:54,080 --> 00:10:58,577
That keeps the demo reproducible without
accounts, paid APIs, or patient uploads.

221
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:03,211
The offline Python pipeline generates
synthetic data, makes patient-level splits,

222
00:11:03,211 --> 00:11:07,221
fits the scaler and classifier on training
people, selects thresholds on

223
00:11:07,221 --> 00:11:09,782
validation people, and exports versioned JSON.

224
00:11:09,905 --> 00:11:14,083
Browser parity fixtures compare the TypeScript
calculation with Python outputs.

225
00:11:14,206 --> 00:11:18,822
The real benchmark uses a separate local
pipeline and exports aggregate evidence

226
00:11:18,822 --> 00:11:19,111
only.

227
00:11:19,233 --> 00:11:21,331
A digital-twin claim also needs care here.

228
00:11:21,453 --> 00:11:25,917
This prototype updates a virtual patient state
with simulated sensor observations.

229
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:30,308
It has no physical patient connection, no
validated physiology simulator, and no

230
00:11:30,308 --> 00:11:31,695
autonomous treatment loop.

231
00:11:31,818 --> 00:11:35,409
Reproducibility requires more than a
screenshot of a good result.

232
00:11:35,531 --> 00:11:39,553
The repository needs the preprocessing logic,
the split protocol, the model

233
00:11:39,553 --> 00:11:43,735
configuration, the selected thresholds, and
the exported artifacts used by the

234
00:11:43,735 --> 00:11:44,272
interface.

235
00:11:44,394 --> 00:11:48,026
The scaler must be fitted on training people
rather than the entire dataset.

236
00:11:48,148 --> 00:11:50,850
Threshold selection belongs on validation
data.

237
00:11:50,972 --> 00:11:54,400
Test results should be reported only after
those choices have been fixed.

238
00:11:54,522 --> 00:11:58,622
Browser parity checks address a different
question: whether the exported model is

239
00:11:58,622 --> 00:12:01,051
evaluated consistently by TypeScript and
Python.

240
00:12:01,174 --> 00:12:04,071
They do not validate the statistical
assumptions of the model.

241
00:12:04,193 --> 00:12:08,145
The architecture keeps runtime inference local
to the browser for the synthetic

242
00:12:08,145 --> 00:12:08,845
demonstration.

243
00:12:08,968 --> 00:12:13,065
The separate retrospective benchmark is an
offline computation that publishes

244
00:12:13,065 --> 00:12:14,076
aggregate evidence.

245
00:12:14,199 --> 00:12:18,579
No real subject trace is necessary for a
reviewer to inspect those reported

246
00:12:18,579 --> 00:12:19,047
metrics.

247
00:12:19,169 --> 00:12:23,066
The term digital twin here describes an
updating virtual state, with the

248
00:12:23,066 --> 00:12:24,743
limitations shown on the slide.

249
00:12:24,866 --> 00:12:30,523
The synthetic evaluation includes 240 people,
split 144 for training, 48 for

250
00:12:30,523 --> 00:12:32,831
validation, and 48 for testing.

251
00:12:32,953 --> 00:12:37,826
The held-out comparison uses the same 30,248
eligible windows.

252
00:12:37,948 --> 00:12:42,763
PR-AUC here means average precision, not
trapezoidal area under a plotted curve.

253
00:12:42,886 --> 00:12:51,292
Fused PR-AUC is 0.3205 compared with 0.2837
for dynamic-only, 0.1776 for static-

254
00:12:51,292 --> 00:12:56,441
only, and 0.0773 for constant persistence
scores.

255
00:12:56,564 --> 00:13:02,513
The fused patient-bootstrap interval is about
0.2731 to 0.3581.

256
00:13:02,636 --> 00:13:06,994
Those correlated windows are not 30,248
independent patients.

257
00:13:07,116 --> 00:13:10,234
The frozen synthetic threshold is 0.15.

258
00:13:10,356 --> 00:13:16,526
It produces 944 false alerts over 336 patient-
days, about 2.81 per day.

259
00:13:16,648 --> 00:13:23,483
It warns 293 of 334 forecastable episodes,
with median lead time of 30 minutes and

260
00:13:23,483 --> 00:13:25,650
window precision of 27.2%.

261
00:13:25,773 --> 00:13:28,474
The episode count and alert burden belong
together.

262
00:13:28,596 --> 00:13:32,299
Alert thresholds were selected separately for
each model, so workload is not

263
00:13:32,299 --> 00:13:33,371
matched across models.

264
00:13:33,493 --> 00:13:37,982
Average precision summarizes how precision
changes as a score threshold varies.

265
00:13:38,104 --> 00:13:41,581
Its baseline depends strongly on how common
the positive outcome is.

266
00:13:41,703 --> 00:13:45,838
That is why prevalence and the common
evaluation denominator belong beside the

267
00:13:45,838 --> 00:13:46,739
model comparison.

268
00:13:46,861 --> 00:13:50,297
Overlapping windows from one person's glucose
trace are correlated.

269
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:54,649
Treating every window as an independent
patient would exaggerate the amount of

270
00:13:54,649 --> 00:13:56,655
independent information in the study.

271
00:13:56,777 --> 00:14:00,888
Resampling whole people is one way to reflect
this clustering in uncertainty

272
00:14:00,888 --> 00:14:01,429
estimates.

273
00:14:01,551 --> 00:14:04,408
The alert workload answers another practical
question.

274
00:14:04,530 --> 00:14:08,636
A model may warn many episodes and still
create too many interruptions for a

275
00:14:08,636 --> 00:14:09,500
useful workflow.

276
00:14:09,623 --> 00:14:13,746
The cooldown prevents every consecutive
positive window from being counted as a

277
00:14:13,746 --> 00:14:16,356
new alert, but it does not eliminate false
alerts.

278
00:14:16,478 --> 00:14:20,033
Different models can select different
thresholds on validation data, so an

279
00:14:20,033 --> 00:14:23,203
apparent sensitivity advantage may come with a
different workload.

280
00:14:23,326 --> 00:14:27,320
The persistence trajectory comparison is a
regression comparison and should remain

281
00:14:27,320 --> 00:14:29,561
separate from the event classification result.

282
00:14:29,683 --> 00:14:34,441
The real feasibility study has 60 training, 20
validation, and 20 held-out people.

283
00:14:34,564 --> 00:14:41,093
There are 20,687 eligible test windows, with
event prevalence about 9.95%.

284
00:14:41,215 --> 00:14:47,262
Fused PR-AUC is 0.3422 and dynamic-only is
0.3398.

285
00:14:47,385 --> 00:14:50,225
The difference is about 0.0024.

286
00:14:50,347 --> 00:14:57,177
Its paired patient-bootstrap interval runs
from about minus 0.00248 to plus

287
00:14:57,177 --> 00:14:59,545
0.00758 and includes zero.

288
00:14:59,667 --> 00:15:02,932
We have not established a meaningful fusion
benefit on these data.

289
00:15:03,054 --> 00:15:10,600
At the validation-selected threshold of 0.20,
window precision is 28.9% and recall

290
00:15:10,600 --> 00:15:11,428
is 50.5%.

291
00:15:11,550 --> 00:15:17,687
The model warns 302 of 326 forecastable
episodes, with a 45-minute median lead.

292
00:15:17,810 --> 00:15:23,637
It also produces 754 false alerts, or about
2.97 per recorded patient-day.

293
00:15:23,759 --> 00:15:27,024
The denominator includes gaps and periods
already above range.

294
00:15:27,146 --> 00:15:30,231
It is recorded-span time, not verified sensor
uptime.

295
00:15:30,354 --> 00:15:32,459
Alerts have a 120-minute cooldown.

296
00:15:32,582 --> 00:15:36,822
Episodes and alerts are counted separately,
and one alert can warn more than one

297
00:15:36,822 --> 00:15:38,254
episode inside its horizon.

298
00:15:38,376 --> 00:15:42,408
These details explain why event sensitivity
alone would overstate usefulness.

299
00:15:42,530 --> 00:15:45,754
The study is retrospective, small, and
geographically limited.

300
00:15:45,876 --> 00:15:47,525
It cannot validate care in India.

301
00:15:47,647 --> 00:15:49,761
The paired interval is especially important.

302
00:15:49,883 --> 00:15:53,869
Comparing two separately rounded point
estimates can make a tiny difference look

303
00:15:53,869 --> 00:15:55,115
more decisive than it is.

304
00:15:55,237 --> 00:15:57,498
Here the interval for the difference includes
zero.

305
00:15:57,620 --> 00:16:01,975
The reasonable interpretation is that this
study has not established a meaningful

306
00:16:01,975 --> 00:16:05,039
advantage for fusion over dynamic glucose
features alone.

307
00:16:05,161 --> 00:16:09,491
That conclusion is compatible with both models
having discrimination above the

308
00:16:09,491 --> 00:16:10,490
constant baseline.

309
00:16:10,613 --> 00:16:15,086
It is also compatible with both being
unsuitable for clinical use at the measured

310
00:16:15,086 --> 00:16:15,803
alert burden.

311
00:16:15,926 --> 00:16:19,084
Window recall and episode warning rate are
different quantities.

312
00:16:19,207 --> 00:16:23,424
Many overlapping opportunities may exist
before one episode, and one alert may

313
00:16:23,424 --> 00:16:26,127
cover more than one episode in its future
horizon.

314
00:16:26,250 --> 00:16:30,118
Counting those quantities carefully avoids
accidental inflation of success.

315
00:16:30,241 --> 00:16:34,108
The denominator for false alerts per day
includes the full recorded span,

316
00:16:34,108 --> 00:16:36,492
including gaps, rather than confirmed uptime.

317
00:16:36,614 --> 00:16:40,717
The result should therefore be read with that
exact denominator and not converted

318
00:16:40,717 --> 00:16:42,997
into an unsupported sensor reliability claim.

319
00:16:43,119 --> 00:16:47,288
Before clinical use, this would need
independent local validation, verified

320
00:16:47,288 --> 00:16:51,568
feature timing, clinician review of the event
definition and acceptable alert

321
00:16:51,568 --> 00:16:54,292
burden, and ethics, privacy, and security
review.

322
00:16:54,414 --> 00:16:57,401
A larger model alone would not resolve those
questions.

323
00:16:57,523 --> 00:17:00,209
The public demo collects no real patient
records.

324
00:17:00,331 --> 00:17:03,930
The real source files remain outside the
repository and recording.

325
00:17:04,052 --> 00:17:08,666
We make no claim of regulatory compliance,
diagnostic ability, treatment benefit,

326
00:17:08,666 --> 00:17:09,520
or safe dosing.

327
00:17:09,643 --> 00:17:13,832
The next research question is whether a
carefully timed and externally tested

328
00:17:13,832 --> 00:17:17,641
model can reduce false alerts while retaining
useful advance warnings.

329
00:17:17,763 --> 00:17:19,600
Our present results do not answer that.

330
00:17:19,722 --> 00:17:23,477
A credible next step would begin with a
specified clinical question and a

331
00:17:23,477 --> 00:17:25,484
prospective data availability contract.

332
00:17:25,606 --> 00:17:29,584
Researchers would need to decide which inputs
are genuinely available at the

333
00:17:29,584 --> 00:17:33,510
forecast time, which population is
represented, and what actions, if any, a

334
00:17:33,510 --> 00:17:35,343
reviewer would take after an alert.

335
00:17:35,465 --> 00:17:39,440
They would need to establish acceptable alert
burden with relevant clinicians

336
00:17:39,440 --> 00:17:43,415
rather than selecting a threshold solely
because it maximizes a retrospective

337
00:17:43,415 --> 00:17:44,189
summary metric.

338
00:17:44,312 --> 00:17:48,817
Independent local validation matters because a
small geographically limited source

339
00:17:48,817 --> 00:17:50,686
cannot stand in for care in India.

340
00:17:50,808 --> 00:17:55,021
Privacy and security review also need to cover
the actual deployment, access

341
00:17:55,021 --> 00:17:58,292
rules, retention policy, and data flows of a
future system.

342
00:17:58,414 --> 00:18:02,095
None of those requirements are satisfied just
by using a synthetic demo.

343
00:18:02,217 --> 00:18:06,159
For this entry, the narrower achievement is a
working, inspectable research

344
00:18:06,159 --> 00:18:09,522
prototype that communicates these limits
instead of hiding them.

345
00:18:09,644 --> 00:18:12,721
Original project code uses the MIT license.

